# Build context exclusions for the Sentinel image ONLY.
#
# Honoured by BuildKit, which looks for a file named `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore`
# and uses it INSTEAD of the root `.dockerignore`. The classic builder has no such
# mechanism and falls back to the root file, which is why the root file is a floor
# and never excludes a path any Dockerfile COPYs.
#
# What this context must contain, and why:
#
#   go.mod, go.sum   the module definition; `go mod download` runs against these
#                    before any source is copied, so the dependency layer is cached
#                    independently of application edits.
#   cmd/             cmd/sentinel/Dockerfile line 67.
#   internal/        cmd/sentinel/Dockerfile line 68.
#
# Everything else is dead weight in the upload. The root file cannot exclude them
# (it must stay correct for the agent build too), so this file is where the
# Sentinel's leanness actually lives.
#
# This is the structural fix for the defect that made the Sentinel unbuildable:
# the root `.dockerignore` used to exclude `internal/` and `cmd/`, so
# `COPY internal/ internal/` failed with `"/internal": not found` on a
# repository where the directory plainly existed. Per-image scoping removes the
# class of error entirely — neither image can now starve the other.

# The Python agent is a separate image with a separate build. Nothing in the Go
# build reaches into it.
agent/

# Not compiled, not copied, not needed to produce /bin/sentinel.
tests/
deploy/
docs/
scripts/
.opencode/

# Documentation and governance files. Excluded rather than merely unused: the
# upload happens on every build and these are ~200 KB of prose per pass.
*.md

# Local scratch that a developer may have left in the worktree.
probe_*.go
tmpdump/
